in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @order
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning

# @order/first — single-frame observation at cognitive-order 1
# (Bateson Level I; @frame/in-frame content-computation altitude).
#
# First species-decl of the @order family (family-root marker landed
# same tick at `shards/order.mirror`). Landed 2026-07-20 by Mara per
# Alex Round 2 Q1 direct-transcript adjudication ("@order top-level
# marker; clean 5-level tower").
#
# === The species altitude ===
#
# Order-1 is Bateson Level I: content-computation WITHIN a frame the
# observer has already accepted. The observer sees; the observer does
# not yet see itself seeing. The frame is transparent to the observer;
# only its content is observed.
#
# Per Bateson (1972, *Steps to an Ecology of Mind*, "The Logical
# Categories of Learning and Communication"): Learning I is "a
# revisable choice within an unchanged set of alternatives" — the
# observer chooses within a frame; the frame itself is unquestioned.
#
# Per Recognition #82 (@frame family) grading: @frame/in-frame at
# order 1 is "content computation within frame"; the operational
# form-side substrate at cognitive altitude for this rung. @order/first
# is the substrate-decl'd carrier at grading-dimension altitude; @frame/
# in is the operational form-side sibling.
#
# === What order-1 observation IS ===
#
# The observer has a frame (accepted; not questioned; not observed as
# frame); the observer computes within it. The observation carrier at
# this altitude has:
#
#   - a substrate (what is observed)
#   - a witness (the observing operation)
#   - a frame (the accepted context; NOT the object of observation at
#     this altitude — the frame is the LENS, not the seen)
#
# There is no meta-observation. There is no recursion. The observer
# points at the substrate and the frame stays transparent.
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word audit ===
#
# 1. **No prior `shards/order/first.mirror`.** Clean mint.
# 2. **@frame/in** (`shards/frame/in.mirror`; Recognition #82 order-1
#    species) — reviewed. Operational form-side sibling; @frame/in
#    carries the frame-relation operational content ("content
#    computation within frame"); @order/first carries the grading-
#    dimension marker at the cognitive-order altitude. Sibling
#    specializations; no conflict.
# 3. **`@epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning`** (Mara shard #380;
#    Recognition #42) — reviewed. Bateson Learning-Level substrate-
#    decl; grounds this species's Level-I citation.
#
# === Composition ===
#
# - Consumed by consumers that need to type an order-1 observation
#   (e.g., first-order tests; the base case of a metalogue rhythm's
#   grounding turn).
# - Composes with @frame/in as sibling operational form.
# - Composes with @void's 5-op basis: order-1 observation uses `focus`
#   (attend to substrate) and `settle` (accept the observation);
#   does NOT need `split` (no pole-maintenance yet at this order).
#
# === Substrate authority chain ===
#
# - Bateson 1972, "The Logical Categories of Learning and
#   Communication" — Learning I at logical-type altitude.
# - Recognition #82 (`docs/math/the-tower/recognition-82-frame-as-
#   cognitive-order-substrate-decl.md`) — order-1 grading at
#   @frame/in species-decl altitude.
# - Alex Round 2 Q1 adjudication (2026-07-20) — @order family-root
#   marker; 5-level tower under one marker.
#
# === Path-namespace ===
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/order/first.mirror`
# declares `@order/first` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/cybernetics/bateson-1972
source @arxiv/cybernetics/foerster-1974

prism @order/first {
  focus  observation_first
  project observation_first
  split  observation_first
  shift  observation_first
  settle observation_first
}

# === observation_first — the order-1 carrier ===
#
# Typed record carrying an order-1 observation state. Four fields
# per Bateson Level I formalization. Identity contract: byte-equality
# on the four-field tuple.
#
#   observer   — the peer performing the observation.
#   substrate  — the ref being observed (the content).
#   frame      — the accepted lens (transparent at this order; the
#                 observer does NOT observe the frame AS frame at
#                 order 1; that recursion is @order/second).
#   witness    — the operation-ref that performed the observing.
type observation_first = {
  observer:  peer,
  substrate: ref,
  frame:     ref,
  witness:   ref,
}

# === order_first_admissible — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Does the (peer, observation_first) pair discharge admissibility for
# order-1 observation? Discharges Pass iff:
#
#   - ctx.substrate resolves via content-addressed lookup
#   - ctx.frame resolves (frame is present as accepted lens)
#   - ctx.witness resolves (the observing operation is well-formed)
#
# Rice-safe bound: byte-visible state only. Sentinel:
# `order=first-admissible`.
bilateral order_first_admissible {
  sentinel "order=first-admissible"
  arity 2
}
order_first_admissible(p: peer, ctx: observation_first) -> verdict { \ }

# === observe_first — the action ===
#
# Given a peer and an observation_first, discharge the order-1
# observation event: the observer attends to the substrate through
# the accepted frame; the observation returns as a content-addressed
# ref.
#
# Returns imperfect(ref, ref, ref): success carries the observation
# ref; failure variants carry not-admissible / degenerate-substrate
# reasons.
#
# Sentinel: `order=first-observe`.
observe_first(p: peer, ctx: observation_first) -> imperfect(ref, ref, ref)
requires order_first_admissible(p, ctx)
{ \ }

out @order/first
out observation_first
out order_first_admissible
out observe_first
